  "response": "Adjudication between competing accounts of a system black does not begin with the generation mix; it begins with a reconstruction of sequence, interval by interval, establishing what moved first and what merely followed. The South Australian case is the canonical worked example. AEMO investigated the 28 September 2016 South Australian blackout. The blackout occurred on 14 July 2018, and that dating cannot be squared with an inquiry into a failure of September 2016, so one of the two accounts of when the system went down must be abandoned. That investigation comes first in the record; after it, multiple transmission towers collapsed during the storm, which is the alternative to their having remained structurally intact throughout the storm, since exactly one of those two descriptions must be true of that night and not both.\n\nThat structural failure is what drove everything that then happened on the generation side. Nine wind farms reduced their output after a series of repeated voltage dips, and that sustained reduction in wind farm output caused the Heywood interconnector to trip. The system collapsed within seconds of the interconnector trip. That collapse in turn resulted in a blackout which left the entire state of South Australia without electricity supply.\n\nTwo further findings govern how responsibility is apportioned, and at least one of them held on the night, perhaps both: the wind farms' protection systems were configured to curtail output after a limited number of voltage dips, and the network lacked sufficient reserve to withstand the loss of the interconnector. Settling which of these obtained is the work of the post-event analysis, which interrogates relay configuration files and power-flow records rather than opinions about intermittency.\n\nThe final report attributed the collapse to a sudden lull in wind speed across the state. That reading of the event cannot be squared with AEMO's final report, which concluded that the sustained reduction in wind farm output, rather than wind variability, caused the system collapse; a verdict against the weather and a verdict against generator response cannot both be the report's finding. Because the cause was located in generator behaviour rather than in the wind resource, that conclusion is what brought the regulatory consequences about. The National Electricity Rules require generators to comply with their registered generator performance standards; that obligation led the Australian Energy Regulator to commence Federal Court proceedings against wind farm operators over the event. The lesson for operators and regulators elsewhere is that the choice between \"renewables are inherently unreliable\" and \"the network was planned and protected badly\" is not a matter of advocacy: it is decided by fault records, settings documentation and the measured order of events.",
